      devanagari: 'परायतीनामन्वेति पाथ आयतीनां प्रथमा शश्वतीनाम् ।\nव्युच्छन्ती जीवमुदीरयन्त्युषा मृतं कं चन बोधयन्ती ॥',
      transliteration: 'parāyatīnāmanveti pātha āyatīnāṁ prathamā śaśvatīnām\nvyucchantī jīvamudīrayantyuṣā mṛtaṁ kaṁ cana bodhayantī',
      translation: 'She follows the path of the dawns that have gone, the first of the endless dawns still to come. Shining out, she rouses the living — but she wakes no one who is dead.',
      commentary: 'Here the beauty turns, without warning, into something that catches in the throat. For seven verses the poet has praised her light; now, in a single line, he names the one thing that light cannot do. She rouses the living — jīvam udīrayanti — and she wakes no one who is dead. The same dawn that lifts you from sleep every morning will, one morning, rise over your stilled body and not reach you. Nothing in the verse is bitter. It is stated the way the hymn states everything: as simple observation, the way it is. And notice the frame the poet sets around it — she is "the first of the endless dawns to come," walking "the path of the dawns that have gone." She is the constant; we are what passes through her light for a while. The Upanishads will later build an entire philosophy on exactly this distinction — the deathless witness and the mortal that appears within it. Here it is already, three centuries earlier, felt rather than argued, at the edge of a single sunrise.'
