      devanagari: 'ईयुष्टे ये पूर्वतरामपश्यन्व्युच्छन्तीमुषसं मर्त्यासः ।\nअस्माभिरू नु प्रतिचक्ष्याभूदो ते यन्ति ये अपरीषु पश्यान् ॥',
      transliteration: 'īyuṣṭe ye pūrvatarāmapaśyanvyucchantīmuṣasaṁ martyāsaḥ\nasmābhirū nu praticakṣyābhūdo te yanti ye aparīṣu paśyān',
      translation: 'Gone now are the mortals who looked on the dawn of an earlier age, watching her shine. It is we who behold her now — and they too are coming, the ones who will look on her in ages still to be.',
      commentary: 'This is the verse people remember for the rest of their lives, and it is only two lines long. Three tenses of humanity, laid side by side under one unchanging light. The people who watched this same dawn before us — gone. We, the living, watching her now. And the ones not yet born who will watch her when we are the ones who are gone. The Sanskrit is plain and unhurried; it does not reach for grandeur, and that is precisely why it lands. You are, at this moment, one of the "we who behold her now." The poet three thousand years ago was one too — he is now among "the mortals who looked on the dawn of an earlier age," exactly as his verse said he would be. The hymn quietly folds you into a procession that stretches out of sight in both directions, and asks nothing of you except to notice that you are in it. Not to fear it. To notice it. That noticing — that the light is old and shared and will outlast you, and that this makes the morning more precious rather than less — is the whole gift of the poem.'
