      translation: 'From days without beginning the goddess Dawn has shone; and this same light she reveals today, the bountiful one. So will she shine through the days still to come — unaging, undying, she moves on by her own power.',
      commentary: 'If verse eleven is the mortals\' side of the ledger, this is the dawn\'s. Ajarāmṛtā — "unaging, undying" — the single word the whole hymn has been circling. She shone before the first day anyone can name; she shines this morning, the same; she will shine through every morning after the last of us. And the phrase that seals it: svadhābhiḥ, "by her own power." She needs nothing from us. Our praise does not sustain her; our watching does not keep her rising. This could read as cold — a universe indifferent to the people in it. The hymn refuses that reading. It has spent twelve verses in open-hearted love of exactly this thing that will outlast it. That is the Vedic poet\'s answer to transience, and it is subtler than either despair or denial: love what does not need you, and let its permanence be a comfort rather than a rebuke. The dawn you are grateful for is the same one your great-grandchildren will be grateful for. You are not the point of it — and being grateful anyway, being one link that lifts its eyes, is enough.'
