      translation: 'Rise up! The living breath, the spirit, has come to us again; the dark has gone, the light draws near. She has cleared a path for the Sun to travel — we have arrived where lives are stretched onward.',
      commentary: 'After the long meditation on mortality, the hymn does not end in melancholy — it ends in a command to get up. Udīrdhvam: "rise!" The breath has come back; you were, in sleep, a small rehearsal of the death the poem faced squarely, and now you are returned to the living, handed one more day. "We have arrived where lives are stretched onward" — where existence is extended, given more length. The whole devastating truth of verses eight and eleven resolves here into something almost brisk: yes, the dawn will one day rise without you; therefore rise now, while it is rising with you. The awareness of the end is not made to crush the morning — it is made to sharpen it. This is the same movement the Gita and the Upanishads will inherit: not "life is short, despair," but "life is short, so wake up and live it." The oldest hymn to the dawn already knew that the right response to mortality is not to look away from it, but to get out of bed.'
